{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 48, :comments ["<b>On the seventh day, the prince of the tribe of the children of Ephraim, Elishama the son of Ammihud.</b> Some say it was the seventh day of the dedication of the altar, and some say it was the seventh day of the creation of the world, so that he offered on the day of the Sabbath; and even though it is an offering of an individual, it was a provisional decree (hora'at sha'ah), and so is the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'On the seventh day, the prince of the tribe of the children of Ephraim' — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joseph: You observed the commandment 'You shall not commit adultery' before the Torah was given; by your life, an individual's offering does not override the Sabbath, but your son's offering overrides the Sabbath. Therefore it says: 'On the seventh day, the prince of the tribe of the children of Ephraim.' And not only that, but no tribe intervenes between your two sons in the offering; rather, 'on the seventh day the prince of the children of Ephraim, on the eighth day the prince of the children of Manasseh.' Elishama — 'my El heard' and to his mistress he did not hear. The son of Ammihud — 'my people was his glory,' and not the people of the Egyptian woman. Gamliel said: Joseph bestowed (gamel) kindnesses upon my people. The son of Pedahzur — Zur redeemed him from his straits from the house of the prison, and thus it is written (Psalms 18): ״יגמלני יהוה כצדקי כבור ידי לנגד עיניו״. The way of a man: when he is poor he fears the Holy One, blessed be He; when he is wealthy he trusts in his wealth. But Joseph feared from beginning to end. At his beginning, what does it say? (Genesis 39) ״ואיך אעשה הרעה הגדולה הזאת וחטאתי לאלהים״. And when he became king, he added fear, as it says (ibid. 42) ״את האלהים אני ירא״. When he was a servant, he would bless over his food, and his master would see him moving his lips. He asked him: What are you whispering? He said to him: I am blessing the Holy One, blessed be He, who gives food to every creature. He said to him: Show Him to me. He said to him: Behold, the sun is one of His attendants who serve before Him on high, and you cannot look at it — the Holy One, blessed be He, all the more so! And on his account the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed Himself to him, as it says (ibid. 39) ״וירא אדוניו כי יהוה אתו״. This is what the verse says (Psalms 60): ״לי גלעד ולי מנשה אפרים מעוז ראשי יהודה מחקקי״. 'To me, Gilead' — said Resh Lakish: if the minim say to you that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not revive the dead, say to them: Elijah testifies that He revived the dead, as it says (I Kings 17) ... — so you say, 'to me, Gilead,' for he was of the inhabitants of Gilead. And if they say to you that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not accept penitents — Manasseh testifies that He accepted him in repentance, as it says (II Chronicles 33) ״ויתפלל אליו ויעתר לו וישמע תחנתו וישיבהו ירושלים למלכותו״ — so you say, 'to me, Manasseh.' 'Ephraim is the strength of my head' — if they say to you that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not remember barren women, Elkanah of Mount Ephraim testifies that He remembered Hannah his wife, as it says (I Samuel 2) ״כי פקד יהוה את חנה״ — so you say, 'Ephraim is the strength of my head.' 'Judah is my lawgiver' — if they say to you that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not save from fire, Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah testify that He saved them from the furnace, as it says (Daniel 1) ״ויהי בהם מבני יהודה דניאל חנניה מישאל ועזריה״ — so you say, 'Judah is my lawgiver.' Another interpretation: 'To me, Gilead, and to me, Manasseh.' If a person says: Why did Elijah build an altar on Carmel and offer upon it, when the Temple was standing at that time? And Moses said (Leviticus 17) ״איש איש מבית ישראל אשר ישחט וגו׳ ואל פתח אהל מועד לא הביאו״ — say to him: Everything Elijah did, he did for the sake of the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, and by divine instruction, as it says (I Kings 18) ״ויהי כעלות המנחה ויגש אליהו הנביא וגו׳ ובדברך עשיתי את כל הדברים האלה״ — so you say, 'to me, Gilead,' for he was of the inhabitants of Gilead. 'And to me, Manasseh' — if a person says to you: Why did Gideon offer on a bama when it was forbidden, since Shiloh was still standing? Rabbi Abba bar Kahana said: Seven things did Gideon do: he offered the firstborn bullock, and the set-aside bullock, and built an altar, and cut down trees from the Asherah, and offered at night, through his own hand without a High Priest, and he was of those who officiate — and everything he did, he did by divine instruction, as it says (Judges 6) ״ויהי בלילה ההוא ויאמר לו יהוה קח את פר השור אשר לאביך ופר השני שבע שנים ובנית מזבח ליהוה אלהיך על ראש המעוז הזה במערכה״ — so you say, 'to me, Manasseh.' If a person says to you: David transgressed a prohibition in the Torah — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: David taught the penitents like a scribe who teaches children, as it says (Psalms 51) ״אלמדה פושעים דרכיך״ — so you say, 'Judah is my lawgiver.' If a person says to you: Why did Joshua desecrate the Sabbath at Jericho? Say to him: He did so by divine instruction, as it says (Joshua 6) ״ויאמר יהוה אל יהושע ראה נתתי בידך את יריחו ואת מלכה וגו׳ וסבותם את העיר כל אנשי המלחמה הקיף את העיר פעם אחת כה תעשה ששת ימים, ושבעה כהנים ישאו שבעה שופרות היובלים לפני הארון וביום השביעי תסובו את העיר שבע פעמים״. And how do we know it was the Sabbath? Because there are never seven days without a Sabbath. And furthermore, Joshua did something of his own understanding that he was not commanded: since the wall of Jericho was conquered on the Sabbath, he said: The Sabbath is entirely holy, and everything we conquered on the Sabbath shall be holy to YHWH — 'and all silver and gold and vessels of bronze and iron are holy to YHWH; into the treasury of YHWH they shall come.' Rabbi Berekhiah the priest said: He treated it like an ir ha-niddahat (a city given over to destruction), and an ir ha-niddahat is forbidden for benefit, as it says (Deuteronomy 13) ״ושרפת באש את העיר ואת כל שללה כליל״. Rabbi Yehudah HaLevi said: He treated it like challah, as it is written (Numbers 15) ״ראשית עריסותיכם חלה תרימו תרומה״. Joshua said: Since we conquered it first, let us consecrate its spoil as holy to YHWH — 'into the treasury of YHWH it shall come.' The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Thus you have done — behold, your offering supports your tribe and overrides the Sabbath. Therefore it says: 'On the seventh day, the prince of the tribe of the children of Ephraim.'"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {1 "The midrashic exposition on Psalms 60 introduces multiple biblical figures (Elijah, Manasseh, Gideon, David, Joshua) as proofs for provisional divine dispensations; the text moves through them in sequence, each tied to the words of the verse. Some proper names and attributions are transmitted in abbreviated or variant form in the manuscripts."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:03.732948232Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}